# Weekly Engineering Digest — modelcontextprotocol/servers
**Week of Oct 7–14, 2026** (today: Wed, Oct 14, 2026)

## 1. v1.0.0 release stabilization
The team spent most of the week hardening the `v2/release/1.0.0` branch via cherry-picks. A cluster of filesystem fixes closed race conditions in roots refresh handling — e.g., **#5094** (revoke access when an update leaves no valid root) and **#5097/#5101** (apply only the newest roots refresh, hold calls during any pending refresh). The fetch tool also got missing annotations (**#3572**, closed), and the npm publish pipeline was fixed to use Node 24's bundled npm instead of installing one (**#5104**).

## 2. Dependency hygiene & supply-chain sweeps
Dependabot PRs were replaced with three scheduled issue-filing workflows — dependency-refresh, dependabot-alerts, and an SDK watch — built via **#5065** (closing tracker **#4874**). This week's alert sweep also produced a wave of direct pins for known CVEs: urllib3, pyjwt, cryptography, soupsieve, gitpython, and several npm transitive deps (hono, qs, nanoid, ip-address).

## 3. CI/process guardrails
New automated checks landed: a DCO signoff verifier for PRs (**#4919**, merged as **#5055**), a case-insensitive fix for the GitHub Actions pin-verification script (**#4934**), and a "main-source guard" workflow ported ahead of the next milestone merge (**#5119**).

## 4. Security/safety gaps in reference servers (open issues)
Several issues flag that example servers can leak data or fetch unsafely by default: **#5130** (everything's gzip-resource fetch allows SSRF to private/metadata IPs, and get-env dumps the full process environment) and **#4882** (same get-env exposure, filed separately). **#5129** reports that git tools print non-ASCII paths as octal escapes, breaking round-trips with `git_add`.

## 5. Tool annotation & documentation accuracy
A recurring theme in new issues is mismatched tool-safety annotations: **#5058** (memory's create/add tools marked non-destructive despite dropping untouched data) and **#5059** (git's `git_create_branch` marked non-destructive but silently resets existing branches). Related: **#5151** proposes adding source-path/untrusted flags to read results, and the broader hardening tracker **#4958** covers tool-definition authorization and parameter bounds across reference servers.

---
**Snapshot:** ~130 commits merged this week (mostly release/cherry-pick traffic); 200 open issues total, with the newest batch concentrated on security hardening and tool-annotation correctness for the bundled reference servers (filesystem, git, everything, fetch, memory).

